//! App passwords, API tokens, feed tokens, client configuration and authentication.
//!
//! # Credentials
//!
//! All credentials are generated by the server (PLAN 2.6); the formats are defined in
//! `davhaven_domain::credential_format` and shared with the authenticators:
//!
//! | Kind | Shown once as | Stored |
//! | --- | --- | --- |
//! | App password | `abcd-efgh-…` (nine groups, 180 bits, of which 140 are secret) | HMAC of the canonical text, found by the first two groups |
//! | API token | `dvh_pat_{prefix12}_{secret43}` | HMAC of `secret43`, found by `prefix12` |
//! | Feed token | `dvh_feed_{prefix12}_{secret43}` inside the feed URL | HMAC of `secret43` |
//!
//! The plaintext leaves this module exactly once, in the response that creates the credential; every creation and
//! revocation is audited in the same transaction. A revocation of an app password or of a principal's token bumps the
//! principal's `credential_generation`, which invalidates the verified-credential cache of the authenticators at once.
//! Tenant tokens belong to no principal and have no generation to bump: the authenticator's cache lifetime (five
//! minutes by default) bounds how long a revoked tenant token keeps working on an instance that verified it recently.
//! Feed tokens are looked up again on every request of a feed, so their revocation and rotation need no generation.
//!
//! # Who may mint what
//!
//! `credentials:write` is as powerful as impersonation: a tenant token with it mints an app password or a principal
//! token for any principal of the tenant that can act (not for a group) and acts as that principal (`docs/interop.md`).
//! The rules of minting API tokens, in the order they are checked:
//!
//! * the kind and the scopes must fit: a token of a kind carries only the scopes that kind may hold (`mcp:*` only on
//!   principal tokens), a principal token needs a principal that is not a group, a tenant token has none, and system
//!   tokens are never minted here (the operator mints them, `davhaven admin system-token`);
//! * **a token mints tokens whose scopes are a subset of its own** (PLAN 2.6), with **one exception**: the `mcp:*`
//!   scopes. A tenant token cannot hold them (`Scope::allowed_for`), so under the plain rule no token could ever mint a
//!   token that carries them, and the machine identity of an assistant needs exactly these. A tenant token with
//!   `credentials:write` may therefore put `mcp:*` scopes on a principal token for a *service principal*, **provided it
//!   holds the API scope that reaches the same data**: `events:read` for `mcp:read`, `events:write` for `mcp:write` and
//!   for `mcp:schedule_external` (`api_equivalent`). Every other scope of that token (`calendars:*`, `events:*`) still
//!   has to be one the minter holds. The exception is as narrow as the need: `credentials:write` is already the power to
//!   act as any principal of the tenant that can act (it mints app passwords and principal tokens for them), and the
//!   events scope is what lets the minter itself write the group and managed calendars that MCP reaches (DAV caps
//!   managed calendars at read, MCP does not) and send invitations by mail (`notify_attendees`), so the token reaches
//!   what the service principal owns or is granted and nothing that a provisioning token could not do itself. It does
//!   not extend to users or resources: MCP scopes reach a user only through the operator (`davhaven admin
//!   principal-token`, the internal channel, which is trusted);
//! * `mcp:*` scopes need a tenant that switched MCP on, and an expiry within `security.max_mcp_token_days`;
//! * a minted token never outlives the token that minted it.
//!
//! # Authentication
//!
//! [`AuthServiceImpl`] runs the configured authenticator chain, applies the failure backoff and builds the
//! `RequestContext`. Failures are counted per (login, client address) and per client address in the rate limiter
//! ([`RateKey::AUTH_LOGIN_IP`] and [`RateKey::AUTH_IP`], which the limiter gives their backoff policy; the keys are
//! keyed hashes of the login and of the address ([`bucket_address`]: an IPv6 client is its /64), so the limiter
//! never sees a login or an address); a request whose bucket is exhausted is answered `RateLimited` with
//! `Retry-After` before any credential is verified, so guessing is slowed down without locking real users out. Every
//! refusal is counted in `davhaven_auth_failures_total{mechanism,reason}`; the client always sees the same
//! `Unauthenticated`. The service also refuses what a misbehaving authenticator could let through: a credential that
//! does not fit the channel or the mechanism it was presented on, and the credentials of a suspended or deleted
//! tenant. The tenant is looked up in a short-lived memory (the `TenantDirectory`) and not once per request.
//!
//! Authentication does not decide visibility (ADR-0012, R4): a **valid** credential presented for the path of
//! another tenant is authenticated, and the request fails with `NotFound`, the answer for everything the caller may
//! not see. That is no failed authentication and never counts toward the backoff. The same holds for MCP in a tenant
//! that has not switched it on (the tenant setting `mcp_enabled`, off by default): the endpoint does not exist for
//! that tenant, so a valid principal token with `mcp:*` scopes is answered `NotFound` there.

